# docs/DECISIONS.md is an append-only log (see its own header: "Append-only;
# newest entry at the bottom of each section"). Every task appends its entry at
# the end of the file, so any two branches developed in parallel collide there
# and git stops with a conflict it cannot resolve on its own — even though the
# two entries have nothing to do with each other. This blocked PRs #131 and #132
# simultaneously, and the resolution both times was mechanical: keep both sides.
#
# `merge=union` is git's built-in driver for exactly this shape. On a conflicting
# hunk it keeps the lines from BOTH sides (ours first, then theirs) instead of
# raising a conflict, which is the correct answer for an append-only log.
#
# Caveat, deliberately not papered over: union resolves by concatenation, not by
# understanding. If two branches ever edit the SAME existing entry, union will
# silently keep both variants back-to-back rather than flagging a conflict. That
# is safe here only because entries are append-only and never revised in place --
# a superseding entry is added, the old one is not rewritten. If that convention
# ever changes, this line should be removed.
#
# Entries also stay roughly chronological on their own, since union keeps the
# base branch's lines first and the incoming branch's after.
docs/DECISIONS.md merge=union
